== The Loveliness of Moral Excellency

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[III. Those affections that are truly holy are primarily founded on the loveliness of the moral excellence of divine things. Or, to express it another way, a love to divine things for the beauty and sweetness of their moral excellence is the first beginning and spring of all holy affections.]

Here, for the sake of the less educated reader, I will explain what I mean by the moral excellence of divine things.

It may be observed that the word moral is not to be understood here in the common, popular sense of the word, as when men speak of #emph[morality] and a #emph[moral] behavior, meaning an outward conformity to the duties of the moral law, and especially the duties of the second table; or meaning, at the most, no more than such seeming virtues as proceed from natural principles, as opposed to those virtues that are more inward, spiritual and divine. In that sense the honesty, justice, generosity, good nature and public spirit of many of the heathen are called #emph[moral] virtues, as distinct from the holy faith, love, humility and heavenly-mindedness of true Christians. I say the word moral is not to be understood in this way here.

But to understand rightly what is meant, it must be observed that divines commonly make a distinction between #emph[moral] good and evil and #emph[natural] good and evil. By #emph[moral] evil they mean the evil of sin, or the evil that is against duty and contrary to what is right and ought to be. By #emph[natural] evil they do not mean the evil that is properly opposed to duty, but what is contrary to nature as such, without any regard to a rule of duty. So the evil of suffering is called natural evil: pain and torment, disgrace and the like. These things are contrary to nature as such, contrary to the nature of both bad and good, hateful to wicked men and devils as well as to good men and angels. So too natural defects are called natural evils, as when a child is born deformed, or born without the use of reason. These are natural evils, but they are not moral evils, because they do not properly have the nature of the evil of sin. On the other hand, as by moral evil divines mean the evil of sin, or what is contrary to what is right, so by #emph[moral] good they mean what is contrary to sin: that good in beings who have will and choice by which, as voluntary agents, they are and act as it becomes them to be and to act, or in the way that is most fit and suitable and lovely. By #emph[natural] good they mean a good that is entirely of a different kind from holiness or virtue, namely what perfects or suits nature, considering nature apart from any holy or unholy qualities, and without any relation to any rule or measure of right and wrong.

So pleasure is a natural good; so is honor; so is strength; so is speculative knowledge, human learning and statecraft. So there is a distinction to be made between the natural good that men possess and their moral good; and also between the natural and moral good of the angels in heaven. The great capacity of their understandings, their great strength, and the honorable position they hold as the great ministers of God’s kingdom (for which they are called thrones, dominions, principalities and powers) are the natural good they possess. But their perfect and glorious holiness and goodness, their pure and flaming love to God, to the saints and to one another, is their moral good. So divines make a distinction between the natural and the moral perfections of God. #emph[By] the #emph[moral] perfections of God they mean those attributes which God exercises as a moral agent, or by which the heart and will of God are good, right, and infinitely becoming and lovely: such as his righteousness, truth, faithfulness and goodness; or, in one word, his holiness. By God’s #emph[natural] attributes or perfections they mean those attributes in which, according to our way of conceiving of God, consists not the holiness or moral goodness of God but his greatness: such as his power, his knowledge by which he knows all things, his being eternal, from everlasting to everlasting, his omnipresence, and his fearful and terrible majesty.

The moral excellence of an intelligent, voluntary being is seated more immediately in the heart or will of moral agents. That intelligent being whose will is truly right and lovely is morally good or excellent.

This moral excellence of an intelligent being, when it is true and real, and not only outward, or merely seeming and counterfeit, is #emph[holiness.] So #emph[holiness] includes all the true moral excellence of intelligent beings: there is no other #emph[true virtue] than #emph[real holiness.] Holiness includes all the true virtue of a good man. His love to God, his gracious love to men, his justice, his charity and heart of compassion, his gracious meekness and gentleness, and all the other true Christian virtues he has belong to his holiness. So the holiness of God, in the wider sense of the word, the sense in which Scripture commonly (if not always) uses the word of God, is the same as the moral excellence of the divine nature, or his purity and beauty as a moral agent, including all his moral perfections: his righteousness, faithfulness and goodness. As in holy men their charity, Christian kindness and mercy belong to their holiness, so the kindness and mercy of God belong to his holiness. Holiness in man is only the image of God’s holiness. There are no more virtues belonging to the image than are in the original. Derived holiness has no more in it than is in that underived holiness which is its fountain. There is no more than grace for grace, or grace in the image answering to grace in the original.

There are two kinds of attributes in God, according to our way of conceiving of him: his moral attributes, which are summed up in his #emph[holiness,] and his natural attributes of strength, knowledge and so on, which constitute the #emph[greatness] of God. So there is a twofold image of God in man. There is his #emph[moral] or #emph[spiritual] image, which is his holiness, the image of God’s moral excellence (this image was lost by the fall); and there is God’s #emph[natural] image, consisting in man’s reason and understanding, his natural ability, and his dominion over the creatures, which is the image of God’s natural attributes.

From what has been said it may easily be understood what I mean when I say that a love to divine things for the beauty of their #emph[moral excellence] is the beginning and spring of all holy affections. It has already been shown, under the former head, that the first objective ground of all holy affections is the supreme excellence of divine things as they are in themselves, or in their own nature. I now go further and say more particularly that the kind of excellence of the nature of divine things which is the first objective ground of all holy affections is their moral excellence, or their holiness. #emph[Holy persons,] in the exercise of #emph[holy affections,] love divine things primarily for their #emph[holiness.] They love God, in the first place, for the beauty of his holiness or moral perfection, as supremely lovely in itself. Not that the saints, in the exercise of gracious affections, love God only for his holiness. All his attributes are lovely and glorious in their eyes; they delight in every divine perfection; the contemplation of the infinite greatness, power and knowledge, and the terrible majesty, of God is pleasant to them. But their love to God for his holiness is what is most fundamental and essential in their love. It is here that true love to God begins; all other holy love to divine things flows from this. This is the most essential and distinguishing thing that belongs to a holy love to God, with regard to its foundation. A love to God for the beauty of his moral attributes leads to, and necessarily causes, a delight in God for all his attributes. For his moral attributes cannot exist without his natural attributes: infinite holiness supposes infinite wisdom and an infinite capacity and greatness; and all the attributes of God, as it were, imply one another.

The true beauty and loveliness of all intelligent beings consists primarily and most essentially in their moral excellence or holiness. In this consists the loveliness of the angels; without it, with all their natural perfections, their strength and their knowledge, they would have no more loveliness than devils. It is moral excellence alone that is, in itself and on its own account, the excellence of intelligent beings. It is this that gives beauty to their natural perfections and qualifications, or rather is the beauty of them. Moral excellence is the excellence of natural excellences. Natural qualifications are excellent or otherwise according as they are joined with moral excellence or not. Strength and knowledge do not make any being lovely without holiness, but more hateful; though they make beings more lovely when they are joined with holiness. So the elect angels are the more glorious for their strength and knowledge, because these natural perfections of theirs are sanctified by their moral perfection. But though the devils are very strong and of great natural understanding, they are not the more lovely for it. They are more terrible indeed, but not the more lovely; on the contrary, they are the more hateful. The holiness of an intelligent creature is the beauty of all his natural perfections. And so it is in God, according to our way of conceiving of the divine being: holiness is in a special way the beauty of the divine nature. That is why we often read of the beauty of holiness (Ps 29:2; 96:9; 110:3). This makes all his other attributes glorious and lovely. It is the glory of God’s wisdom that it is a holy wisdom, and not a wicked subtlety and craftiness. What makes his majesty lovely, and not merely dreadful and horrible, is that it is a holy majesty. It is the glory of God’s unchangeableness that it is a holy unchangeableness, and not an inflexible obstinacy in wickedness.

And so a sight of God’s loveliness must begin here. A true love to God must begin with a delight in his holiness, and not with a delight in any other attribute. For no other attribute is truly lovely without this, nor lovely in any other way than as (according to our way of conceiving of God) it derives its loveliness from this. So it is impossible that other attributes should appear lovely in their true loveliness until this is seen; and it is impossible that any perfection of the divine nature should be loved with true love until this is loved. If the true loveliness of all God’s perfections arises from the loveliness of his holiness, then the true love of all his perfections arises from the love of his holiness. Those who do not see the glory of God’s holiness cannot see anything of the true glory of his mercy and grace. They see nothing of the glory of those attributes as any excellence of God’s nature as it is in itself, though they may be affected by them, and love them, as they concern their interest. For these attributes are no part of the excellence of God’s nature, as that nature is excellent in itself, in any other way than as they are included in his holiness, taken more broadly, or as they are a part of his moral perfection.

As the beauty of the divine nature consists primarily in God’s holiness, so does the beauty of all divine things. In this consists the beauty of the saints: that they are saints, or holy ones. It is the moral image of God in them which is their beauty, and that is their holiness. In this consists the beauty and brightness of the angels of heaven: that they are holy angels, and so not devils (Dan 4:13, 17, 23; Matt 25:31; Mark 8:38; Acts 10:22; Rev 14:10). In this consists the beauty of the Christian religion above all other religions: that it is so holy a religion. In this consists the excellence of the word of God: that it is so holy. Ps 119:140: “Your word is completely pure; therefore Your servant loves it.” Verse 128: “I consider all Your precepts right in all things, and I hate every false way.” Verse 138: “The testimonies You have laid down are righteous and altogether faithful.” And verse 172: “My tongue sings of Your word, for all Your commandments are righteous.” And Ps 19:7–10: “The Law of the #smallcaps[Lord] is perfect, reviving the soul; the testimony of the #smallcaps[Lord] is trustworthy, making wise the simple. The precepts of the #smallcaps[Lord] are right, bringing joy to the heart; the commandment of the #smallcaps[Lord] is pure, giving light to the eyes. The fear of the #smallcaps[Lord] is clean, enduring forever; the judgments of the #smallcaps[Lord] are true, being altogether righteous. They are more to be desired than gold, than much pure gold; they are sweeter than honey, than honey from the comb.” In this primarily consists the loveliness and beauty of the Lord Jesus, by which he is the chief among ten thousand and altogether lovely: in that he is “the Holy One of God” (Acts 3:14), and God’s “holy servant” (Acts 4:27), and “the One who is holy and true” (Rev 3:7). All the spiritual beauty of his human nature, consisting in his meekness, lowliness, patience, heavenliness, love to God, love to men, stooping to the lowly and despised, compassion to the miserable, and so on, is all summed up in his holiness. And the beauty of his divine nature, of which the beauty of his human nature is the image and reflection, also consists primarily in his holiness. In this primarily consists the glory of the gospel: that it is a holy gospel, and so bright an outshining of the holy beauty of God and Jesus Christ. In this consists the spiritual beauty of its doctrines: that they are holy doctrines, or doctrines according to godliness. And in this consists the spiritual beauty of the way of salvation by Jesus Christ: that it is so holy a way. And in this chiefly consists the glory of heaven: that it is the #emph[holy city,] the #emph[holy Jerusalem, the habitation of God’s holiness,] and so #emph[of his glory] (Isa 63:15). All the beauties of the new #emph[Jerusalem,] as it is described in the last two chapters of Revelation, are only various representations of this: see Rev 21:2, 10, 11, 18, 21, 27; 22:1, 3.

And so it is primarily on account of this kind of excellence that the saints love all these things. So they love the word of God #emph[because it is completely pure.] It is on this account that they love the saints; and it is chiefly on this account that heaven is lovely to them, and those holy tabernacles of God lovely in their eyes. It is on this account that they love God; and it is primarily on this account that they love Christ, and that their hearts delight in the doctrines of the gospel and sweetly rest in the way of salvation revealed there #footnote[For the right embracing of Christ’s person this also is required: to taste the bitterness of sin as the greatest evil. Otherwise a man will never embrace Christ for his holiness, in him and from him, as the greatest good. For we told you that it is the right embracing of Christ for himself when it is for his holiness. For ask a whorish heart what beauty he sees in the person of Christ: after he has looked over his kingdom, his righteousness and all his works, he will see a beauty in them because they serve his purpose, to comfort him only. Ask a virgin: he will see his happiness in all; but what makes the Lord lovely is his holiness, which is in him to make him holy too. As in marriage, it is the personal beauty that draws the heart. And so I have thought it reasonable that he who loves the brethren for a little grace will love Christ much more. #emph[Shepard]’s parable, P. I. P. 84.].

Under the head of the first distinguishing characteristic of gracious affection, I observed that a new supernatural sense is given to those who are regenerated. It is, as it were, a certain divine, spiritual taste, which is in its whole nature different from any former kinds of sensation of the mind, as tasting is different from any of the other five senses. And I observed that something is perceived by a true saint in spiritual and divine things, in the exercise of this new sense of mind, which is as entirely different from anything natural men perceive in them as the sweet taste of honey is different from the ideas men get of honey by looking at it or touching it. Now what I have been speaking of, namely the beauty of holiness, is that thing in spiritual and divine things which is perceived by this spiritual sense, so different from all that natural men perceive in them. This kind of beauty is the quality that is the immediate object of this spiritual sense; this is the sweetness that is the proper object of this spiritual taste. Scripture often represents the beauty and sweetness of holiness as the grand object of a spiritual taste and a spiritual appetite. This was the sweet food of the holy soul of Jesus Christ (John 4:32, 34): “I have food to eat that you#super[+] know nothing about. … My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me and to finish His work.” I know of no part of the holy Scriptures where the nature and evidences of true and sincere godliness are so deliberately, so fully and so extensively set out and described as in Psalm 119. The psalmist declares his purpose in the first verses of the psalm, and he keeps his eye on this purpose all the way through and pursues it to the end. And in this psalm the excellence of holiness is represented as the immediate object of a spiritual taste, relish, appetite and delight. #emph[God’s law,] that grand expression and outflowing of the holiness of God’s nature, and prescription of holiness to the creature, is represented all through as the food and entertainment, and as the great object of the love, the appetite, the delight and the rejoicing of the gracious nature. That nature prizes God’s commandments #emph[above gold, above pure gold,] and to it they are #emph[sweeter than honey, than honey from the comb;] and that on account of their holiness, as I observed before. The same psalmist declares that this is the sweetness a spiritual taste relishes in God’s law (Ps 19:7–10): “The Law of the #smallcaps[Lord] is perfect … the commandment of the #smallcaps[Lord] is pure … The fear of the #smallcaps[Lord] is clean … the precepts of the #smallcaps[Lord] are right, bringing joy to the heart … the judgments of the #smallcaps[Lord] are true, being altogether righteous. They are more to be desired than gold, than much pure gold; they are sweeter than honey, than honey from the comb.”

A holy love has a holy object. The holiness of love consists especially in this: that it is the love of what is holy, as holy, or for its holiness; so that it is the holiness of the object which is the quality on which it fixes and in which it comes to rest. A holy nature must love that in holy things chiefly which is most agreeable to itself. But surely what is agreeable to a holy nature in divine things, above all else, is holiness, because holiness must be agreeable to holiness above all other things. For nothing can be more agreeable to any nature than itself: holy nature must be agreeable to holy nature above all things. And so the holy nature of God and Christ, and the word of God, and other divine things, must be agreeable above all other things to the holy nature that is in the saints.

And again, a holy nature no doubt loves holy things especially on account of that for which sinful nature has enmity against them. But what sinful nature is chiefly at enmity against in holy things is their holiness. It is for this that the carnal mind is enmity against God, and against the law of God and the people of God. Now it is fair arguing from contraries: from contrary causes to contrary effects, from opposite natures to opposite tendencies. We know that holiness is of a nature directly contrary to wickedness. So, as it is the nature of wickedness chiefly to oppose and hate holiness, it must be the nature of holiness chiefly to tend toward holiness and delight in it.

The holy nature in the saints and angels in heaven, where its true tendency appears best, is chiefly engaged by the holiness of divine things. This is the divine beauty that chiefly engages the attention, wonder and praise of the bright and burning #emph[seraphim] (Isa 6:3): “And they were calling out to one another: ‘Holy, holy, holy is the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts; all the earth is full of His glory.’ ” And Rev 4:8: “Day and night they never stop saying: ‘Holy, Holy, Holy, is the Lord God Almighty, who was and is and is to come!’ ” So too the glorified saints (Rev 15:4): “Who will not fear You, O Lord, and glorify Your name? For You alone are holy.”

And the Scriptures represent the saints on earth as adoring God primarily on this account, and as wondering at and extolling all God’s attributes, either as deriving their loveliness from his holiness or as being a part of it. So when they praise God for his power, his holiness is the beauty that engages them (Ps 98:1): “Sing to the #smallcaps[Lord] a new song, for He has done wonders; His right hand and #emph[holy] arm have gained Him the victory.” So too when they praise him for his justice and terrible majesty (Ps 99:2–3): “Great is the #smallcaps[Lord] in Zion; He is exalted above all the peoples. Let them praise Your great and awesome name—He is #emph[holy!]” Verse 5: “Exalt the #smallcaps[Lord] our God, and worship at His footstool; He is #emph[holy!]” Verses 8–9: “You were a forgiving God to them, yet an avenger of their misdeeds. Exalt the #smallcaps[Lord] our God and worship at His holy mountain, for the #smallcaps[Lord] our God is #emph[holy.]” So too when they praise God for his mercy and faithfulness (Ps 97:11–12): “Light shines on the righteous, gladness on the upright in heart. Rejoice in the #smallcaps[Lord], you#super[+] righteous ones, and praise His #emph[holy] name.” 1 Sam 2:2: “There is no one #emph[holy] like the #smallcaps[Lord]. Indeed, there is no one besides You! And there is no Rock like our God.”

By this, then, all may test their affections, and particularly their love and joy. Various kinds of creatures show the difference of their natures very much in the different things they relish as their proper good, one delighting in what another abhors. Such a difference there is between true saints and natural men. Natural men have no sense of the goodness and excellence of holy things, at least not for their holiness. They have no taste for that kind of good, and so they may be said not to know that divine good, or not to see it; it is wholly hidden from them. But the saints, by the mighty power of God, have it shown to them. They have been given that supernatural, most noble and divine sense by which they perceive it. And it is this that captivates their hearts and delights them above all things. It is the most lovely and sweet thing to the heart of a true saint that is to be found in heaven or earth: what attracts and engages his soul above all else, what he places his happiness in above all things, and what he counts on for comfort and refreshment to his mind in this world and for full satisfaction and blessedness in the next. By this you may examine your love to God, to Jesus Christ and to the word of God, and your joy in them, and also your love to the people of God and your desires for heaven: whether they come from a supreme delight in this sort of beauty, without being moved primarily by your imagined interest in them or your expectations from them. There are many high affections, great seeming love and rapturous joys, which have nothing of this holy relish in them.

In particular, by what has been said you may test your discoveries of the glory of God’s grace and love, and the affections arising from them. The grace of God may appear lovely in two ways: either as #emph[bonum utile,] a profitable good to me, something that greatly serves my interest and so suits my self-love; or as #emph[bonum formosum,] a good that is beautiful in itself and part of the moral and spiritual excellence of the divine nature. It is in this latter respect that the true saints have their hearts affected, and their love captivated, by the free grace of God in the first place.

From what has been said it appears that if persons have a great sense of the #emph[natural] perfections of God, and are greatly affected by them, or have any other sight or sense of God than one that consists in, or implies, a sense of the beauty of his #emph[moral] perfections, it is no certain sign of grace. In particular, men’s having a great sense of the #emph[fearful greatness and terrible majesty of God] is no certain sign; for this is only God’s natural perfection, and men may see it and yet be entirely blind to the beauty of his moral perfection, having nothing of that spiritual taste which relishes this divine sweetness.

It has already been shown, in what was said on the first distinguishing mark of gracious affections, that what is spiritual is entirely different in its nature from all that any graceless person can possibly experience while he remains graceless. But it is possible for those who are wholly without grace to have a clear sight, and a very great and affecting sense, of God’s greatness, his mighty power and fearful majesty. For this is what the devils have, though they have lost the spiritual knowledge of God, which consists in a sense of the loveliness of his moral perfections. They are entirely without any sense or relish of that kind of beauty; yet they have a very great knowledge of the natural glory of God (if I may speak so), or his fearful greatness and majesty. This they behold, and are affected by their apprehensions of it, and so they tremble before him. All will behold this glory of God at the day of judgment. God will make all rational beings behold it to a great degree indeed, angels and devils, saints and sinners. He will display his infinite greatness and fearful majesty to everyone in a most open, clear and convincing way, and in a light that no one can resist, when #emph[he comes in the glory of his Father, and every eye sees him.] When they cry to the mountains to fall on them, to hide them from the face of him who sits on the throne, they are represented as seeing the glory of God’s majesty (Isa 2:10, 19, 21). God will make all his enemies behold this, and live in a most clear and affecting view of it in hell to all eternity. God has often declared his unchangeable purpose to make all his enemies know him in this respect, by so often adding these words to the threats he pronounces against them: “and they will know that I am the #smallcaps[Lord].” Indeed, he has sworn that all men will see his glory in this respect (Num 14:21): “As surely as I live, all the earth shall be filled with the glory of the #smallcaps[Lord].” And this kind of manifestation of God is very often spoken of in Scripture as made, or to be made, in the sight of God’s enemies in this world (Exod 9:16; 14:18; 15:16; Ps 66:3; 46:10; and countless other places). This was a manifestation God made of himself in the sight of that wicked congregation at Mount #emph[Sinai,] affecting them deeply with it, so that all the people in the camp trembled. Wicked men and devils will see, and have a great sense of, everything that belongs to the glory of God except the beauty of his moral perfection. They will see his infinite greatness and majesty and his infinite power, and will be fully convinced of his omniscience, his eternity and his unchangeableness. And they will see and know everything that belongs to his moral attributes themselves, except their beauty and loveliness. They will see and know that he is perfectly just and righteous and true; that he is a holy God, of purer eyes than to behold evil, who cannot look on iniquity; and they will see the wonderful displays of his infinite goodness and free grace to the saints. Nothing will be hidden from their eyes except the #emph[beauty] of these moral attributes, and that beauty of the other attributes which arises from it. And so natural men in this world are capable of having a very affecting sense of everything else that belongs to God, except this alone. #emph[Nebuchadnezzar] had a great and very affecting sense of the infinite greatness and fearful majesty of God, of his supreme and absolute dominion, his mighty and irresistible power, and his sovereignty, and that he and all the inhabitants of the earth were nothing before him. He also had a great conviction in his conscience of God’s justice, and an affecting sense of his great goodness (Dan 4:1–3, 34, 35, 37). And the sense that #emph[Darius] had of God’s perfections seems to have been very much like his (Dan 6:25 and following). But the saints and angels behold the glory of God that consists in the #emph[beauty of his holiness;] and it is this sight alone that will melt and humble the hearts of men, wean them from the world, draw them to God, and effectually change them. A sight of the fearful greatness of God may overpower men’s strength and be more than they can endure. But if the moral beauty of God is hidden, the enmity of the heart will remain in its full strength; no love will be kindled; nothing will be effectual to win the will, but it will remain inflexible. Whereas the first glimpse of the moral and spiritual glory of God shining into the heart produces all these effects, as it were with almighty power, which nothing can withstand.

The sense that natural men may have of the fearful greatness of God may affect them in various ways. It may not only terrify them; it may also elevate them and raise their joy and praise, as their circumstances may be. This will be its natural effect, under the influence of principles of nature alone, when they receive, or suppose they receive, some extraordinary mercy from God. It has already been shown that receiving kindness may, by the influence of natural principles, affect the heart with gratitude and praise to God. But if a person, at the same time that he receives remarkable kindness from God, has a sense of his infinite greatness, and that he himself is nothing in comparison with him, surely this will naturally raise his gratitude and praise all the higher for kindness to one so far beneath him. A sense of God’s greatness had this effect on #emph[Nebuchadnezzar] when he received that extraordinary favor of his restoration, after he had been driven from men and had lived with the beasts. A sense of God’s exceeding greatness raised his gratitude very high, so that he extolled and magnified God in the loftiest terms and called on all the world to do so with him. And much more, if a natural man, at the same time that he is greatly affected by God’s infinite greatness and majesty, holds a strong conceit that this great God has made him his child and special favorite, and has promised him eternal glory in his highest love, this will tend, by the course of nature, to raise his joy and praise to a great height.

So it is beyond doubt that too much weight has lately been laid by many persons on discoveries of God’s greatness, fearful majesty and natural perfection working in this way, without any real view of the holy, lovely majesty of God. And experience abundantly bears witness to what reason and Scripture declare on this matter. For there have been very many persons who seemed to be overpowered by the greatness and fearful majesty of God, and were in consequence elevated in the way just described, who have been very far from showing a Christian spirit and temper in any sort of proportion, or fruits in practice in any way agreeable to it. Their discoveries have worked in a way contrary to the working of truly spiritual discoveries.

Not that a sense of God’s greatness and natural attributes is not exceedingly useful and necessary. For, as I observed before, this is implied in a manifestation of the beauty of God’s holiness. Though that is something beyond it, it supposes it, as the greater supposes the less. And though natural men may have a sense of the natural perfections of God, yet no doubt this is more frequent and common with the saints than with natural men; and grace tends to enable men to see these things in a better way than natural men do. It enables them to see not only God’s natural attributes, but that beauty of those attributes which (according to our way of conceiving of God) is derived from his holiness.
